Common Ansible Errors & Fixes – A DevOps Engineer’s Guide

Working with Ansible is powerful, but let’s be honest – we’ve all hit those frustrating errors. 😅
 Here are some of the most common ones I’ve faced (and how to fix them):

🔹 1. Host unreachable
 👉 Usually caused by wrong IP/hostname or SSH not working.
 ✅ Fix: Check your inventory file, ensure the host is reachable, and SSH keys are set up correctly.

🔹 2. Permission denied
 👉 Happens when Ansible doesn’t have the right privileges to run tasks.
 ✅ Fix: Use become: yes in your Playbook or run with --ask-become-pass.

🔹 3. Module not found
 👉 Error: "The module X was not found in configured module paths."
 ✅ Fix: Ensure the correct module name is used and Ansible version supports it.

🔹 4. YAML syntax errors
 👉 A missing colon or wrong indentation can break your Playbook.
 ✅ Fix: Always validate YAML using ansible-playbook --syntax-check.

🔹 5. Variable undefined
 👉 Error: "Variable 'X' is undefined."
 ✅ Fix: Double-check variable spelling, scope, and defaults in your Playbooks/Roles.

💡 Pro tip: Run with -vvv for verbose logs – it makes debugging much easier.

At the end of the day, debugging Ansible is all about patience + systematic checks. Once you know where to look, fixes become second nature. 🚀
